 **Formulas:**
-The core innovation is the integration of the diffusion prior gradient. Let $p(\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D})$ be the joint probability distribution of image $\mathbf{I}$ and depth $\mathbf{D}$ learned by the diffusion model. The prior term added to the loss is related to the gradient of the log-likelihood:
+The core innovation is the integration of the diffusion prior gradient. Let $P(\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D})$ be the joint probability distribution of image $\mathbf{I}$ and depth $\mathbf{D}$ learned by the diffusion model. The prior gradient is:
 $$
-\nabla_{\theta} \mathcal{L}_{prior} \propto \nabla_{\theta} \nabla_{\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D}} \log p(\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D})
+\nabla_{\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D}} \log P(\mathbf{I}, \mathbf{D})
 $$
-where $\theta$ are the NeRF parameters. The rendered RGBD patches are treated as samples from the NeRF, and their deviation from the learned prior distribution guides the optimization [S1].
+This gradient is backpropagated to the NeRF parameters $\theta$ via the rendering equation [S1].
 
 **Parameters:**
-*   **Input Views:** Sparse configurations (e.g., 3, 6, 9 views) [S1].
-*   **Prior Dataset:** Synthetic RGBD data (e.g., Hypersim) [S1].
+*   **Input Views:** The method is evaluated on 3, 6, and 9 input views [S1].
+*   **Dataset:** Trained on Hypersim (synthetic), evaluated on LLFF [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Domain Gap:** The prior is trained on synthetic data (Hypersim). Performance may degrade if the target scene distribution differs significantly from the synthetic prior [S1].
-*   **Sparse Views:** The method is specifically designed for sparse views; performance with dense views may not show significant improvement over standard NeRF [S1].
+*   **Sparse Views:** The method is specifically designed for sparse views; performance with dense views is not explicitly detailed but standard NeRF priors may be less critical [S1].
+*   **Domain Gap:** The diffusion prior is trained on synthetic data (Hypersim). Performance on real-world data may depend on the similarity to the training distribution [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### SSDNeRF
-SSDNeRF is a method that learns generalizable 3D priors through a single-stage 3D latent diffusion model, jointly optimizing NeRF and diffusion components end-to-end from multi-view images [S2]. Unlike DiffusioNeRF, which uses a separate diffusion model for regularization, SSDNeRF integrates the diffusion process directly into the 3D representation learning [S2]. It enables robust learning even with sparse-view input and supports flexible test-time sampling, allowing 3D reconstruction from arbitrary view counts [S2].
+### [SSDNeRF](11954-ssdnerf.md)
+A unified framework that jointly optimizes a NeRF auto-decoder and a latent diffusion model in a single training stage to learn generalizable 3D priors from multi-view images. Unlike DiffusioNeRF which uses a pre-trained diffusion model for gradient guidance, SSDNeRF blends generative and rendering biases coherently in one stage, enabling robust training on as few as three views and avoiding noisy latent manifolds induced by inverse rendering uncertainty.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On the LLFF dataset, DiffusioNeRF achieves PSNR indicators 1-2 points higher than baseline methods in 3, 6, and 9 input views [S1]. Among three input views, the PSNR index is nearly 1 point higher than ViP-NeRF [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improved geometry reconstruction quality under sparse input views; effective normalization of scene geometry and color [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on a pre-trained diffusion prior, which may not generalize to out-of-domain scenes; increased training complexity due to the diffusion model integration [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When training NeRFs with sparse input views and high-quality geometry reconstruction is critical [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the LLFF dataset, DiffusioNeRF achieves PSNR indicators 1-2 points higher than baselines for 3, 6, and 9 input views. Specifically, with 3 input views, it is nearly 1 point higher than ViP-NeRF [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves geometry reconstruction quality under sparse input views by leveraging learned priors [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained diffusion model on synthetic data, introducing a potential domain gap for real-world scenes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DiffusioNeRF was introduced in February 2023 as a method for geometry regularization in NeRFs [S2]. It leverages the power of denoising diffusion models to provide strong priors for natural scene statistics, addressing the challenges of training NeRFs with limited input views [S1]. The method builds on the observation that diffusion models can effectively model complex joint distributions of RGBD data [S1].
+DiffusioNeRF was introduced in "Methods and Strategies for Improving the Novel View Synthesis Quality of Neural Radiation Field" by S. Fang et al. (2024) [S1]. It addresses the challenge of novel view synthesis with sparse input views by utilizing a denoising diffusion model to learn priors for scene geometry and color.
 
